Operating System Software (OSS): Provides an interface between the EEC hardware and the AS. The OSS
capabilities include the bootstrap software, scheduler, Built-In-Tests (PBIT, CBIT), System configuration data
file (system parameters/constant file), device driver software for the hardware resources such as memory
devices (e.g., Flash, EEPROM), interface devices (e.g. RS-232, ARINC 429, CAN, Discrete I/Os, ADC),
watchdog timers, etc.
Application Software (AS): Per Figure 2, the AS includes the control law algorithms which are executed based
on the inputs acquired by the EEC hardware and processed by the OSS, and the ASI for handling the
communication between AS Control Laws and OSS. The outputs generated by the AS control law algorithm
functions are fed to the OSS via the ASI to drive the EEC hardware accordingly. The AS capabilities also
include fault detection, accommodation and annunciation, communication with the Engine Display System and
the engine mounted DCU.
The AS is divided further into two components:
a.
Application Software –Control Laws (AS-CL): This component includes a subset but is not limited to
the following components:– Control Laws (CL): Capabilities include control law algorithms, Inputs reasonability checks, Fault
accommodation, Fault annunciation, etc.– Parts Library (PL): Contains operators that are used to design AS-CL components.– Power Settings (PS): This component is used in turbo-fan and turbo-prop applications only. It
adjusts the control system speed references based on the ambient conditions and the amount of
thrust (turbo-fans) or SHP (turbo-prop) requested by the throttle position.– Time Limited Dispatch (TLD): This component classifies the faults detected by the control system
software into categories, which relate to the probability of occurrence of a Loss of Thrust Control
(LOTC) event. This classification allows an engine with a control system with detected faults, to be
dispatched for some time period, before the fault has to be corrected.– Monitoring & Diagnostics: Monitors and schedules the data to be recorded in the DCU in order to
provide information on the engine history, engine events and control system faults.– Cruise-Control: The Cruise Speed Control function is designed to control the airspeed of the aircraft
by varying the control system references. This component is used in turbo-fan and turbo-prop
applications only.– Low Cycle Fatigue (LCF): The automated LCF counting functions provide an algorithm for
evaluating life usage of engine components related to engine spool speeds. Maintenance personnel
use the information stored in the DCU to schedule engine maintenance.– Creep: The Creep function provides a mechanism for evaluating engine component blade creep
incurred during engine operation as a result of day to day aircraft operations. Maintenance personnel
use the information stored in the DCU as an aid to schedule engine maintenance.
NOTE: All the aforementioned AS-CL components, including the libraries/core described in MED 248, can be
developed as standalone projects following the process described hereafter.
NOTE: Not all applications use TLD, LCF, Creep, Power Setting, Cruise-Control, or Monitoring and
Diagnostics.
b.
Application Software Interface (ASI): The purpose of the ASI is to provide interface between AS-CL
and the OSS via common memory allocation such as shared memory, APEX, etc. The ASI capabilities
also include the maintenance interface which is used to transmit data between the EEC and the Ground
Support Equipment (GSE), etc.
The source code of AS-CL and ASI are integrated, compiled and linked together to form an AS executable image.
