Software Verification Process Activities
The software verification activities for Application Software CSCI is performed as per SVP to meet RTCA/
DO-178B/C level-A software objectives and are described here for completeness only. Refer to the PSAC for
software verification process activities detailed responsibilities. The following are the typical software verification
activities:
• Unit Testing
• Integration Testing includes– Software Integration (SI) Testing– Hardware/Software Integration (HSI) Testing
• Reviews and analysis
Unit Testing (UT): The purpose of unit testing is to verify the implementation of computer software units (CSU)
satisfy their low-level requirements. This testing is also called Module testing. Code reviews, preparation of unit test
cases, test procedures and test data peer review are the activities performed in Unit testing.
Software Integration (SI) Testing: Integration test ensures that the code for CSUs that constitute computer software
components (CSC) interact correctly with each other and satisfy the software high-level requirements and software
architecture. Preparation of software integration test cases, test procedures and test data peer review are the
activities performed in integration testing.
Hardware/Software Integration (HSI) Testing: The purpose of HSI testing is to verify correct operation of the AS
CSCI with the other relevant CSCI(s) in the target computer environment (HWCI) and correct operation of
hardware/software interfaces to satisfy the software high-level requirements.
For Software changes involving the use of a Core Library, the credit that can be taken for the use of the library is
specified in MED 248 and in the specific Core Library User Guide. Refer to the Core Library System and Software
Checklists found in the specific Core Library User Guide which will detail if re-execution of verification tests must be
performed or if any core verification credit can be sought.
